.TH CLOG_FLUSH 3 "2026-08-26" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_flush \- block until queued log records are durably written
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <clogger.h>

void clog_flush(clog logger);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Blocks the calling thread until every record queued through logger
(or any sibling handle sharing the same underlying target) as of the
moment this call is made, plus any partially-filled aggregation
buffer, has been durably written via write(2).

A no-op that returns immediately for a synchronous (non-async)
logger, i.e. one created without a clog_async_cfg_t (see
clog_open_fd_mp(3) / clog_open_file_mp(3)).

A record submitted by some other thread concurrently with, but
strictly after, this call is not guaranteed to be included.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
logger  Logger handle.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Safe to call on any still-open handle at any time, including
concurrently with ordinary log_*() calls on the same or a sibling
handle.
If the underlying async queue cannot accept the flush request (a rare
allocation failure), this function returns immediately without
blocking rather than waiting for an acknowledgment that will never
arrive.
In a forked child, a logger whose async mode has fallen back to
synchronous logging (see clog_open_fd_mp(3)) treats this call as the
same no-op a synchronous logger's own call would be.
Asserts if logger is CLOG_INVALID, stale, or already closed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_open_fd_mp (3),
.BR clog_open_file_mp (3),
.BR clog_close (3)
